@local/dsh-restart-button README · dsh-restart-button：[简体中文](README.md) · **English**

A one-click **reload / restart** button for the [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai) desktop app, sitting at the bottom of the sidebar.

Installing or updating a plugin normally means quitting the app and opening it again. This plugin puts a button in the sidebar that does it for you — and, more importantly, tries **not** to restart at all first.

@local/dsh-restart-button README · Why not just restart?：Because `app.relaunch()` is not the cheap operation it looks like.

When you replace a plugin package, the Harness's own HMR layer can evict the changed modules and start them again **inside the running host process**. Turns in flight, subagents, terminals, queued messages, unsent drafts — all survive, because nothing is killed.

A full restart is the fallback for the cases where that is impossible (a module that cannot be evicted, a package that moved on disk), not the default.

@local/dsh-restart-button README · Why not just restart?：So the button defaults to the cheap path. The expensive one is offered in exactly two cases:

1. **The Harness says a restart is required** — `hmr.reconcileProfile()` returns `restartReason: 'module-comparison' | 'package-moved'`.
2. **You press it a second time** — the reload went through, you pressed again, therefore the reload did not solve what you were doing.

@local/dsh-restart-button README · Why not just restart?：The second one is decidable without guessing: a reload that actually changed Client code reloads the page, which resets the button. A button still sitting there waiting for you means nothing was swapped, so a second press has nothing cheaper to offer. So within one page, the first press reloads and the second press restarts — and the label flips to "Full restart" before you do it.

Accidental presses are filtered: a second click within 500 ms counts as a double click and is ignored.

---

@local/dsh-restart-button README · Install：A successful install reports `application: "applied"`. The button appears at the bottom of the sidebar immediately — no restart needed to install *it*.

To update later: `git pull`, then press the button.

